WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.830
Film Courage: I asked you this before but
when did you get “good” at acting.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:11.890
When did you think you weren’t really mastering
the craft but honing in and what we talked

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:15.770
about earlier with the Bill Duke falling back
into the abyss.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:22.980
Rhomeyn Johnson, Actor: Well like I said before
you never master, you get good or whatever.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.279
There’s always something to learn.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:34.050
And I always take classes and stay in classes
because of the fact that this is a work-in-progress.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:41.210
But I think when I felt that I was really
connecting…I don’t like to say I really

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.210
got good.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:48.440
I like to say when I really starting connecting
to what I was doing I think that was finally

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:59.990
when I stopped trying to be perfect see and
it kind of hurt me (that whole thing of perfection),

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.090
hurt me musically.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:08.210
It didn’t hurt me as much as an actor and
it hasn’t hurt me as much as an actor but

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:14.520
it really hurt me musically because I got
into a thing (as far as singing and musical

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.270
stuff) trying to be too perfect.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.590
Trying to make each note sound….laaaaa!

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.149
[Sings]…whatever.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:27.850
And that gets you in your head and once you
get into your head with anything (especially

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.859
this) you are in trouble.

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:38.460
It’s like what Bill Duke was saying about
stage fright, that’s that ego and everything

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.289
when you are out here when you are out judging
yourself in what you are doing.

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:49.569
See that’s the same thing as getting in
your head “That didn’t sound good.”

00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:51.600
Or “That note could sound better.”

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.560
Why are you performing?

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.090
That’s not good.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:10.179
When you drop that perfection mindset and
accept the fact that you are not perfect then

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:18.760
you free yourself to the point now where you
can be the actor or be the character whatever,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.269
you free yourself.

00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:27.410
Then you are able to able to really let go
and have fun and explore, you free yourself.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:35.189
Once you free yourself in anything, that’s
when you get good or great or whatever.

00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:44.069
The best actors like he was saying [Bill Duke],
the best actors are the ones who free themselves

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:51.629
to just be like Meryl Streep, Phillip Seymour
Hoffman, Daniel Day Lewis.

00:02:51.629 --> 00:03:00.599
I mean he is so into that he IS that person
that’s freedom.

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:04.549
Even though he is so in and locked in he is
still free.

00:03:04.549 --> 00:03:13.390
He is free of Daniel Day Lewis who is a very
nice guy and what not normally.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:18.069
I mean Daniel Day Lewis wouldn’t take a
bowling ball and throw it at someone trying

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:19.150
to hurt them.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:25.069
Not Daniel Day Lewis but that character or
the person he is being would but he tried

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:26.530
to do that.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:33.680
So that’s when to me when you try to do
that and that is when you finally recognize

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.150
and accept yourself for who you are and the
fact that you are not perfect.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:46.090
Because most actors who try to do everything
perfect and …that’s bad because then you

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.109
are acting.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:50.209
And somebody said (I don’t know who it was)
said “Whatever you do, don’t ever let

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:51.209
them see you act.”

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:56.440
Don’t ever let them see that because then
you are in trouble because then people are

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.800
looking at you like “Ahhhh?”

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.830
You’ve seen people perform or act and you
were like “Okay…that was okay.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:05.830
I didn’t buy it.”

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:14.849
But the person who like you said Christian
Bale in VICE he was all the way in.

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:17.699
He wasn’t trying to be perfect.

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:24.800
He was so busy trying to be a flawed person…Dick
Cheney was a flawed person.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.470
Christian Bale didn’t have to try and be
perfect.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:35.500
If we get religious or something, the only
person who was closest to being perfect was

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.500
what?

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.500
Jesus?

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.460
Jesus Christ or somebody?

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:40.580
You know what I’m saying.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:47.640
And Jesus Christ if you read The Bible and
any of that wasn’t perfect.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.830
He had flaws too.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:57.590
Once you get to the point of accepting yourself
and saying “Okay this is who I am, I’m

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:02.639
going to just get into this as me and I’m
just going to just let us go and be free and

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:04.000
just let go.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.470
That’s when you’ve got some beautiful
stuff going on.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:14.810
Film Courage: It was great too in VICE because
Dick was this restrained, there was sort of

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:19.430
this anger bubbling at the surface whereas
the character of his wife (played by Amy Adams

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:24.680
who was great) was emotional and power-driven
and it was a great combination to see the

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:33.949
two of them together and I left that film
thinking it was going to be this sexy, rock

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:39.660
n’ roll version of it and it took me an
hour to feel normal (whatever normal is) but

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.330
it was pretty emotional.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:06:07.440
It really affected me.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:58.970
Rhomeyn: Being an actor being like how Bill
Duke says it’s like falling into darkness

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:05.139
backwards to do that it’s like that whole
experiment if you’ve ever been in one of

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:12.759
those psychiatric…where you trust things
you know [trust fall] where somebody has to

00:07:12.759 --> 00:07:20.580
catch you, you’ve got to let yourself…and
to be honest with you it’s hard to let yourself

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.050
go when somebody has got to catch you.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:28.449
I mean I don’t care how great of an actor
you are, it’s hard to fall back and you’ve

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:32.360
got somebody trying to catch you that you
don’t know where you don’t know if they

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:33.560
are going to be able to catch your big fat
a$$ (like me).

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:41.449
It’s like me I’m still…I can’t because
I’m scared I’m going to hit my damn head.

00:07:41.449 --> 00:07:48.020
But what he said was once you’re able to
basically free fall backwards then you are

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:49.020
there.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.499
So there’s a little more work I need to
do.

